A||||10||Homicidal||How-To-Guide on how to replace Stock 4 X 10's!||||10||
Z||000000||Homicidal||02-21-2001||07:44 PM||||I plan to buy a new set of Pioneer 4X10's.  The replacement seems pretty cut and dry, but what do I need to know to replace them?<P>Also, what color is which on the Fiero (+ or -)<P>Thanks for the help in advance!||||reg||10
Z||000001||MrPBody||02-21-2001||08:51 PM||||It's pretty straightforward.<P>You might want to use some kind of little hook -- like a bent piece of welding rod -- to pull up your grilles, so you don't crack your dash like I did by prying up the grilles.<P>If you don't want to cut your speaker wires, you can remove the connectors from your stock speakers and solder them to your new ones.<P>I don't know what wire's positive or negative.  Oreif's probably the man for this.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||02-21-2001||08:55 PM||||Just take a flathead and pry the covers open, there is 4 bolt/screews holding the speaker in, take them out, then splice the wire after the connection:<P>Dk Green       Right-Front Speaker + <BR>Lt Green       Right-Front Speaker - <BR>Grey #2        Left-Front Speaker + <BR>Tan            Left-Front Speaker - <P>then connect the wires, screew the speakers back in, pop the covers on and turn it  up <P><BR>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<P>  <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||02-21-2001||09:59 PM||||Most car audio retail shops have speaker wiring adapters so you don't have to cut and splice the wires. I bought some at Best Buy or if you order from Crutchfield's, the adapters are provided for free. Just plug and play then.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||02-21-2001||10:15 PM||||You may have a little trouble getting at the forward most screws, at least I do when I take my pioneers out and try to get them back it.  The angle of the glass doesnt help with this.  If you can find one of those "stubby" screwdrivers, it would help you out alot.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hank is Here||02-21-2001||10:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>You may have a little trouble getting at the forward most screws, at least I do when I take my pioneers out and try to get them back it.  The angle of the glass doesnt help with this.  If you can find one of those "stubby" screwdrivers, it would help you out alot.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mine were held in with hex head screws not normal screws.  Just took a scoket and 1/4 drive rachet and worked perfect on the front ones.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||RossT||02-21-2001||11:01 PM||||Make sure you buy the speaker wire adapter from crutchfield.  Costs 5 bucks for a set of 2.  Makes it much easier.  Read this link too. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005268.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005268.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||GarethWright||02-22-2001||01:55 AM||||Heres a Handy Hint I came up with for getting the front screws out: Even with the stubby screwdriver, the glass gets in the way, What I did was use a Phillips-head-bit with the hex base (like from drill adapters) and stick that in a small socket wrench.  You will still hit the glass with the top of the wrench, but you have a better angle on the screw, and are less likely so strip the screw head. <P>PS I have the Pioneer 4X10s (blue cones I think) and they sound Great.||||reg||1
Z||000008||MontanaFiero||02-22-2001||02:44 AM||||If you go with 4X10 speakers again, make sure they don't stick up too high. The Kenwood 4X10's have a 3/4 inch spacer on the top and the tweater sits up that high and you won't be able to put the covers back on.<P>Some stereo shops have an adapter plate for 4X10 holes that will allow you to put a 4" woofer and a tweeter in it for less distortion.<P>just my 2 cents<P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000009||RossT||02-22-2001||02:59 AM||||The new kenwoods 4x10 kfc-415c that I just installed fit just fine. It may have been an older model that had the fit problems.  Also the new kenwoods no longer have a paper tweeter.  The highs sound really good.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||02-22-2001||09:35 AM||||Remove old speakers, installation is reverse of removal  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I used that little craftsman 90* angle screwdriver to get at the front screws on my 4x10's.  Still a bear to get at...<P>||||reg||1
